                description="&lt;h3>A slow day in the Napa Valley&lt;/h3>&#xa;&lt;p>The Napa Valley is fifty kilometres of vineyards an hour north of San Francisco, and its famous names can be crowded and formal. We go instead to three small, family-run wineries where the person pouring is often the person who made the wine. A driver takes care of the road, so everyone can taste.&lt;/p>"
                description_x0040_ContentType="text/html"
                description_x0040_LastModified="{Date}2020-10-19T11:54:42.730-07:00"
                difficulty="Beginner"
                difficulty_x0040_LastModified="{Date}2020-10-19T11:54:42.732-07:00"
                gearList="&lt;ul>&#xa;&lt;li>Shoes for walking on gravel and between vines&lt;/li>&#xa;&lt;li>A sun hat, and a layer for the cool cellars&lt;/li>&#xa;&lt;li>A photo ID showing that you are 21 or older&lt;/li>&#xa;&lt;/ul>"
                gearList_x0040_ContentType="text/html"
                gearList_x0040_LastModified="{Date}2020-10-19T11:54:42.734-07:00"
                groupSize="{Long}8"
                groupSize_x0040_LastModified="{Date}2022-05-27T14:49:02.095-07:00"
                itinerary="&lt;h3>Morning: sparkling wine&lt;/h3>&#xa;&lt;p>We leave the city at nine and start, as one should, with bubbles: a small producer on the cooler southern edge of the valley, with a walk through the vines and a look at the cellar.&lt;/p>&#xa;&lt;h3>Midday: a hillside estate&lt;/h3>&#xa;&lt;p>Up a winding road on the eastern side, a family has farmed the same slope for three generations. We taste their Cabernet from the barrel and from the bottle, and have a picnic lunch under the oaks with the valley below.&lt;/p>&#xa;&lt;h3>Afternoon: the old vines&lt;/h3>&#xa;&lt;p>The last visit is to a grower who works Zinfandel vines planted a century ago. A final tasting in the garden, and we are back in San Francisco by six.&lt;/p>"
